  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 AM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nAdditional shortwave energy diving south out of the western Great \nLakes today will deepen the broad 500mb trough in place across the \nOhio Valley and Appalachians. It will also provide the necessary \nlift to produce some isolated/widely scattered showers this \nafternoon, particularly across southern IL/southwest IN/northwest \nKY. A final lobe of vorticity will sink south towards the Ohio River \non Friday, which may allow a few isolated showers again up in the \nEvansville Tri-state region (although we currently have silent \nPoPs). The majority of the shower activity should remain east of our \ncwa on Friday.\n\nThe coldest airmass so far this Fall season will be in place the \nnext 4-5 days as 850mb temps bottom out in the -5 to -7 C range \nFriday into Saturday. This will lead to highs stuck in the 40s/50s \nand lows dropping into the 20s/low 30s through Monday. We were \ncertainly due for a cooler stretch like this.\n\nWhile a mainly dry forecast is expected this weekend through mid \nnext week, there is a cutoff upper low that develops across the \nSouthern Plains and moves into the lower Mississippi Valley Sunday \ninto Monday. The moisture from this system could spread \nprecipitation far enough north to clip portions of southeast MO and \nwest KY Sunday evening into early Monday. Ensemble guidance \ncurrently keeps it primarily south of our border though. After this \nsystem slides by, mid level ridging builds in mid next week which \nwill lead to temperatures returning to near or even slightly above \nnormal.",
